# Cloudflare Pages custom headers.
# https://developers.cloudflare.com/pages/configuration/headers/
#
# Without this file every asset ships with Pages' default
# "Cache-Control: max-age=0, must-revalidate", so each page navigation
# re-fetches all four stylesheets over the network; on a flaky phone
# connection those requests can fail and the page paints unstyled
# (seen on iOS Safari). A real max-age lets the browser reuse its
# cached copy instead of depending on the network every time.
#
# Deliberately NO rules for: *.html (rebuilt store/index must show up
# immediately), /js/* apart from /js/vendor/* (catalog edits to js/products.js must reach
# shoppers right away; the admin editor also fetches it with
# cache:'no-store'), and /admin + /api (auth-guarded, served through
# Pages Functions, which _headers does not apply to anyway).
#
# css and js: the pages name every one of their own scripts and stylesheets
# as file?v=<fingerprint of its content> (stamp_assets.py; the GitHub Action
# keeps it current), so a changed file is a new address and reaches every
# visitor on their next page load. That matters because caching is long:
# a day for css here, and Cloudflare's zone-wide "Browser Cache TTL" (4 hours
# by default) for every .js file, data included -- which is NOT set in this
# file, and which made a deploy look like it had changed nothing before the
# fingerprints existed.
# images: site chrome (heroes, favicons, logos), the blog cover images in
# images/blog, and the copies of product images and partner logos that
# fetch_images.py keeps in images/products and images/partners. Those copies
# are named after a hash of their original address, so a changed image is a
# new file and nothing goes stale with the catalog. Replacing an image file
# under the same name reaches repeat visitors within a week; give it a new
# name to show it at once.
# fonts and js/vendor (jQuery, GSAP, Swiper): third-party files whose
# version is in the file name, and the site's fonts -- they never change in
# place, so browsers may keep them for a year.

# Every static page and file: no MIME sniffing, no framing by other sites
# (clickjacking), and the browser-default referrer behaviour made explicit
# (merchants still see the visit came from mycampuskorner.com).
/*
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  X-Frame-Options: SAMEORIGIN
  Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin

/css/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=86400, stale-while-revalidate=604800

/images/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=604800, stale-while-revalidate=604800

# The copies of retailers' images are third-party files served from this
# site's own origin, so they are sandboxed: one opened directly in a tab
# (an SVG can carry script) runs nothing and can reach nothing. Pages show
# them with <img>, which this does not affect. fetch_images.py also refuses
# any SVG containing script.
/images/products/*
  Content-Security-Policy: default-src 'none'; img-src 'self' data:; style-src 'unsafe-inline'; sandbox

/images/partners/*
  Content-Security-Policy: default-src 'none'; img-src 'self' data:; style-src 'unsafe-inline'; sandbox

/images/categories/*
  Content-Security-Policy: default-src 'none'; img-src 'self' data:; style-src 'unsafe-inline'; sandbox

/images/blog-covers/*
  Content-Security-Policy: default-src 'none'; img-src 'self' data:; style-src 'unsafe-inline'; sandbox

/documents/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=604800, stale-while-revalidate=604800

/fonts/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable

/js/vendor/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable
